306 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 164 0 R
/E (Massive Software Reuse: By elevating VirtIO directly over shared memory, the Zephyr RTOS interacts with the Linux backend as if it were interacting with physical hardware. Standard VirtIO drivers can be used without writing custom, proprietary RPC protocols from scratch.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
307 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 164 0 R
/E (True Bare-Metal Determinism: Removing the heavyweight hypervisor eliminates VM-exits and hypervisor scheduling overhead. The Cortex-R5 core runs natively on bare-metal, achieving desired determinism for its local tasks)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
308 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 164 0 R
/E (Direct Hardware Signaling: Uses physical Inter-Processor Interrupts \(IPIs\) for direct core-to-core signaling.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
309 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 164 0 R
/E (Security Vulnerabilities: The PMM operates in Linux user-space with direct access to the pre-shared memory pool. Without strict hardware separation, a compromised "rich OS" could expose the RTOS memory to external vulnerabilities)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
310 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 164 0 R
/E (Explicit Memory Copies: The lack of shared-memory awareness in standard frontend drivers mandates an explicit memcpy into the shared bounce buffers, limiting throughput.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
311 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 164 0 R
/E (Inherited Jitter on the Communication Path: While the RTOS is deterministic, the inter-processor data exchange is not. Waking up the Linux PMM relies on the Linux OS scheduler \(kernel module\) introducing latency and the typical jitter of general-purpose operating systems.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
